==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bekenen}}, {{m|enm|beknen}}, {{m|enm|becnen}}, {{m|enm|beknien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|bēacnian}}, {{m|ang|bēcnian}}, {{m|ang|bīecnan||to signal; beckon}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*bauknōn}}, {{m|gmw-pro|*bauknijan||to signal}}, from {{m|gmw-pro|*baukn||signal; beacon}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|bōknian}}, {{cog|goh|bouhnen}}, {{cog|non|bákna}}. More at {{l|en|beacon}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɛkən/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-beckon.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkən|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[wave]] or [[nod]] to [[somebody]] with the [[intention]] to [[make]] the [[person]] [[come]] [[closer]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=His distant friends, he '''beckons''' near.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|I|iv|passage=It '''beckons''' you to go away with it.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[seem]] [[attractive]] and [[inviting]]
#* {{quote-song|1=en|year=2016|lyricist=Brandon Michael Armstrong; Joshua Ross Kaufman; Justin Isaac Rubenstein; Patrick Nolen McHale|artist=Jack Jones|title=Into the Unknown|album=Over the Garden Wall (Original Television Soundtrack)|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bFQAcogDYvs|text=How the gentle wind / '''Beckons''' through the leaves / As autumn colors fall}}

